/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  cosmoSAC03_ethanol_water_predictive -- what a PREDICTIVE model is, shown
  on one system beside a FITTED one and the measurements.

  A FITTED model (NRTL here) has binary parameters regressed to measurements
  of THIS pair: it can only be as good as those data, and it says nothing
  about a pair nobody measured.  A PREDICTIVE model computes the activity
  coefficients from properties of the PURE molecules alone.  COSMO-SAC does it
  from each molecule's sigma profile -- the distribution of screening charge
  over its surface, from a quantum-chemistry calculation -- and a handful of
  UNIVERSAL constants fitted once, to many systems, and never to this one.
  Nothing in this case was adjusted to ethanol-water.

  THE PREDICTIVE MODEL: COSMO-SAC variant LVPP-HFTZVP-2018 -- the 2002
  equations (Lin & Sandler, Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 41 (2002) 899) with the
  constants Ferrarini, Flores, Muniz & Soares fitted to the LVPP sigma-profile
  database's GAMESS HF/TZVP profiles (AIChE J. 64 (2018) 3443).  The paper
  leaves two choices open; the reading the engine uses was SELECTED on Lin &
  Sandler's Table 2 and every run announces that (record:
  docs/design/cosmo-sac-lvpp-hftzvp-2018.md).  The profiles are LVPP's (MIT);
  which file stands for water and for ethanol is stated in each component's
  record (the archive holds water twice and ethanol as three conformers).

  THE FITTED MODEL: the ethanol-water NRTL pair in constant/parameters/NRTL
  (Gmehling & Onken, DECHEMA Vol. I Part 1), the pair the sibling case
  eval_nrtl_catalogue scores on these same points (aad 2.04 K).

  THE MEASUREMENTS: eleven 1-atm bubble temperatures, the pedagogical subset
  of the DECHEMA ethanol-water isobar already used by fitNRTL01 and
  eval_nrtl_catalogue.

  WHAT TO LOOK AT.
    1. cosmo_score -- the prediction scored against the measurements (aad,
       rms, max in K; the per-point parity in parity_cosmo.csv).  Nothing is
       fitted: `mode evaluate`.
    2. txy_cosmo / txy_nrtl -- the two T-x-y curves at 1 atm on the same
       grid.  Plot T_bubble and y_eq_ethanol against x[ethanol] from both
       CSVs with the measured points of parity_cosmo.csv on top.
  WHAT IT SHOWS (measured 2026-11-11, the numbers this case's golden pins).
  On these eleven points the prediction scores aad 0.98 K, max 1.78 K.  The
  catalogue NRTL scores 2.04 K on the same points in eval_nrtl_catalogue,
  but that case seals its own pure-component records, so the two scores are
  not from one frame; the two T-x-y curves below ARE (same components, same
  vapour pressures, only gamma differs).  And look at the SHAPE: the NRTL curve has the minimum-boiling
  azeotrope (T_bubble lowest near x = 0.9), and the COSMO-SAC curve has NONE
  -- its lowest bubble point is pure ethanol.  The prediction gets the
  average temperature right and the qualitative fact a distillation designer
  needs most wrong: gamma-inf of water in ethanol is 1.85 against NRTL's
  2.58, and the curve never turns down into an azeotrope.  One system proves nothing either
  way; the family-by-family comparison on the catalogue's own measured bubble
  points is in docs/design/cosmo-sac-lvpp-hftzvp-2018.md section 6.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        cosmo_score;
        type        fitParameters;
        mode        evaluate;
        residual
        {
            kind        T_bubble;
            components  ( ethanol water );
            P           1.01325 bar;
            data
            (
                0.05  361.95    0.10  359.95    0.20  358.05
                0.30  357.30    0.40  356.85    0.50  356.20
                0.60  355.30    0.70  354.50    0.80  353.30
                0.90  351.85    0.95  351.45
            );
        }
        output      { parity  parity_cosmo.csv; }
    }

    { name txy_cosmo;  type propertyScan1D;
      vary  { variable x[ethanol]; from 0.0; to 1.0; n 21; }
      state { T 350 K; P 1.01325 bar; composition { ethanol 0.5; water 0.5; } }
      properties ( T_bubble y_eq_ethanol gamma_ethanol gamma_water );
      output { file txy_cosmo.csv; } }

    { name txy_nrtl;   type propertyScan1D;
      thermo { equilibrium { liquid { activityModel { model NRTL; } } } }
      vary  { variable x[ethanol]; from 0.0; to 1.0; n 21; }
      state { T 350 K; P 1.01325 bar; composition { ethanol 0.5; water 0.5; } }
      properties ( T_bubble y_eq_ethanol gamma_ethanol gamma_water );
      output { file txy_nrtl.csv; } }
);
